<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        BUILD verified live (2026-08-27): a real `mvn package` against this exact file, on the
        fedora-runner systemd-nspawn container on acer (see packaging/ci/fedora-runner/), produces
        a real nspawnmgr-0.3.0-1.noarch.rpm via .gitea/workflows/build.yml's `rpm-package` job,
        including the same bundled debian-minimal.tar.gz/postgresql-minimal.tar.gz consistency the
        Arch/SteamOS packages already have. INSTALL still unverified - only the build side is
        proven so far, same posture as the Arch package before its own SteamOS/QEMU verification
        pass.

        RPM package for production nspawnmgr deployment - the RPM/Fedora-host equivalent of
        ../nspawnmgr-deb (a .deb) and ../nspawnmgr-arch (an Arch PKGBUILD). Uses
        org.codehaus.mojo:rpm-maven-plugin - CORRECTION to this project's own earlier planning
        assumption (confirmed live 2026-08-26): this plugin is NOT entirely Redline-based/
        pure-Java the way jdeb is for .deb - it genuinely shells out to a real `rpmbuild` binary
        for the actual build step (`rpm-build` package), and separately to plain `rpm` for one
        macro lookup (see this file's own <vendor> comment below) unless overridden. A real
        RPM/Fedora host (or equivalent - `rpm-build` installed) is required to build this, same
        "needs the real distro's own native tooling" story ../nspawnmgr-arch/PKGBUILD already has
        with `makepkg`, just discovered the hard way here since the plugin's own reputation
        suggested otherwise going in. Confirmed live: this module can never fully build on a
        non-RPM host (e.g. Windows) - packaging/ci/fedora-runner/ exists specifically to give this
        a real build/test environment, same reasoning as the Arch runner.

        Like the .deb/Arch packages, this ships already-built artifacts (the WARs, the vendored
        guacd/Tomcat/Guacamole binaries) rather than building anything from a downloaded source
        tarball. Build this LAST, same prerequisites as ../nspawnmgr-deb - see
        tools/scripts/build-all.sh.

        No <parent>: same reasoning as ../nspawnmgr-deb's own pom - this module just packages
        already-built WARs into an .rpm, it doesn't need the Spring Boot parent at all.

        Reuses ../nspawnmgr-deb/'s privileged-scripts/, vendor/ binaries, and sudoers files by
        reference rather than duplicating them - same cross-module pattern
        ../nspawnmgr-deb-bundled/pom.xml and ../nspawnmgr-arch/PKGBUILD already use. `Requires:`
        translated from ../nspawnmgr-deb/debian/control's `Depends:` line - `openssh-server`/
        `openssl`/`dnsmasq`/`systemd-container` all keep the exact same package names on Fedora
        (confirmed real live 2026-08-26 via the packaging/ci/fedora-runner/ test runner - none of
        these needed a name translation).

        Three files need renaming on the way in (source filename differs from where the package
        should install it) - rpm-maven-plugin's <mapping>/<source> model doesn't reliably support
        per-file renaming the way jdeb's <dst> does, so a maven-antrun-plugin <copy file= tofile=>
        step stages renamed copies into target/rpm-staging/ first (generate-resources phase, ahead
        of the rpm-maven-plugin's own package-phase execution), and the rpm mappings below point
        at those staged copies instead of the originals directly.
    -->
    <groupId>com.nspawnmgr</groupId>
    <artifactId>nspawnmgr-rpm</artifactId>
    <version>0.4.0</version>
    <packaging>pom</packaging>
    <name>nspawnmgr-rpm</name>
    <description>RPM package for production nspawnmgr deployment (Fedora/RHEL)</description>

    <properties>
        <!-- 2.3.0 is the real latest release on Maven Central (confirmed live via search.maven.org
             - an earlier guess of 2.4.0 doesn't exist at all, "Could not find artifact"). -->
        <rpm.plugin.version>2.3.0</rpm.plugin.version>
        <staging.dir>${project.build.directory}/rpm-staging</staging.dir>
        <debdir>${project.basedir}/../nspawnmgr-deb</debdir>
        <nspawnmgr.war>${project.basedir}/../../target/nspawnmgr.war</nspawnmgr.war>
        <auth.war>${project.basedir}/../../auth/target/auth.war</auth.war>
        <root.war>${project.basedir}/../../root-wizard/target/ROOT.war</root.war>
        <guacamole.jdbc.drivers.dir>${project.basedir}/../../target/guacamole-jdbc-drivers</guacamole.jdbc.drivers.dir>
    </properties>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <!-- Stage the three rename cases before the rpm-maven-plugin execution below reads
                 them - see this file's own top-of-file comment for why. One plugin, one step:
                 antrun's <copy file= tofile=> renames directly from each original source, no
                 intermediate non-renamed copy needed. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-antrun-plugin</artifactId>
                <!-- Explicit version required - this module has no <parent> providing
                     dependencyManagement for it (same reasoning as ../nspawnmgr-deb's own pom -
                     these packaging modules just package already-built WARs, no Spring Boot
                     parent needed). 3.2.0 confirmed real/current via search.maven.org. -->
                <version>3.2.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>stage-renamed-files</id>
                        <phase>generate-resources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>run</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <target>
                                <mkdir dir="${staging.dir}"/>
                                <copy file="${debdir}/vendor/guacamole-1.5.5.war" tofile="${staging.dir}/guacamole.war"/>
                                <copy file="${debdir}/debian/nspawnmgr.sudoers" tofile="${staging.dir}/nspawnmgr_exec"/>
                                <copy file="${debdir}/vendor/README.md" tofile="${staging.dir}/guacd-bundle-README.md"/>
                            </target>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>rpm-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${rpm.plugin.version}</version>
                <extensions>true</extensions>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>generate-rpm</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>rpm</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
                <configuration>
                    <!-- Not documented on the goal's own reference page, but confirmed live
                         required - "The parameters 'sourceEncoding' ... are missing or invalid"
                         with no other value set. -->
                    <sourceEncoding>UTF-8</sourceEncoding>
                    <name>nspawnmgr</name>
                    <!-- Must match this module's own <version> above (stripped of any -SNAPSHOT
                         suffix during a dev cycle) - same "keep these two in sync by hand" trap
                         ../nspawnmgr-deb/pom.xml's own comment already warns about for the .deb's
                         control-file Version:. -->
                    <version>0.4.0</version>
                    <release>1</release>
                    <group>Applications/Internet</group>
                    <packager>nspawnmgr packaging &lt;packaging@nspawnmgr.invalid&gt;</packager>
                    <!-- Confirmed live: without this, the plugin shells out to a real `rpm`
                         binary (`rpm -E %{_host_vendor}`) just to look up a default - fails
                         outright on a Windows build host with no rpm binary at all, and is a
                         fragile, environment-dependent default even on a real Linux host. -->
                    <vendor>nspawnmgr packaging</vendor>
                    <!-- TODO: fill in once a public repo URL exists - same placeholder as
                         ../nspawnmgr-arch/PKGBUILD's own url="". -->
                    <url></url>
                    <summary>Web manager for systemd-nspawn containers with Apache Guacamole access</summary>
                    <description>nspawnmgr provides a web UI for creating, starting, stopping, and accessing systemd-nspawn containers via Guacamole (SSH/RDP). Deploys as nspawnmgr.war and auth.war into its own self-hosted machine, alongside guacamole.war.</description>
                    <license>Apache-2.0</license>
                    <needarch>noarch</needarch>
                    <requires>
                        <require>openssh-server</require>
                        <require>openssl</require>
                        <require>dnsmasq</require>
                        <require>systemd-container</require>
                        <!-- NOT a straight translation of ../nspawnmgr-deb/debian/control's own
                             Depends: line (which has no iptables entry either) - confirmed live
                             (real Fedora 43 Cloud Edition install, packaging/nspawnmgr-rpm/
                             verification): the privileged-scripts/nspawnmgr-outbound-*.sh scripts
                             (the per-container outbound-internet-access toggle) call `iptables`
                             directly and unconditionally, and Fedora's minimal Cloud image simply
                             doesn't ship that binary at all - native nftables only. Debian's own
                             Depends: line has the same latent gap in principle, it just doesn't
                             bite there because `iptables` is near-universally already present on
                             a real Debian host. `iptables-nft` is Fedora's nft-backed package that
                             actually provides /usr/bin/iptables (confirmed live: the plain
                             `iptables` package name doesn't exist on Fedora 43 - `iptables-nft`
                             does, and command creation of the NSPAWNMGR-OUTBOUND chain succeeds
                             immediately after installing it). -->
                        <require>iptables-nft</require>
                    </requires>

                    <mappings>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/nspawnmgr</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${nspawnmgr.war}</location></source>
                                <source><location>${auth.war}</location></source>
                                <source><location>${root.war}</location></source>
                                <source><location>${staging.dir}/guacamole.war</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/vendor/guacd-bundle.tar.gz</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/vendor/guacamole-auth-jdbc-1.5.5.tar.gz</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/vendor/apache-tomcat-9.0.120.tar.gz</location></source>
                                <!-- Consistency with the published .deb (the bundled variant,
                                     nspawnmgr-deb-bundled, not the "online" nspawnmgr-deb) and
                                     with the Arch/SteamOS packages (see those PKGBUILDs' own
                                     comments on this exact point, raised again for RPM after
                                     initially being missed here) - no network needed for the app
                                     machine or a PostgreSQL DB machine at install time. Zero
                                     script changes needed: nspawnmgr-bootstrap-app-machine.sh/
                                     nspawnmgr-bootstrap-db-machine.sh already check for
                                     BUNDLED_DEBIAN_MINIMAL/BUNDLED_POSTGRESQL_MINIMAL at these
                                     exact /usr/share/nspawnmgr/ paths. -->
                                <source><location>${debdir}/vendor/debian-minimal.tar.gz</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/vendor/postgresql-minimal.tar.gz</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/guacd.service</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/tomcat9.service</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/dnsmasq-nspawnmgr.conf</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/70-nspawnmgr-bridge.netdev</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/70-nspawnmgr-bridge.network</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/debian/nspawnmgr-ci.sudoers</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <!-- Fedora/RHEL-only: source for the SELinux policy module
                                 post-install.sh compiles+loads to fix a real AVC denial (confirmed
                                 live: systemd-machined can't watch a container's own cgroup.events
                                 file under Enforcing, causing "Failed to register machine: Access
                                 denied" on every single container start) - see that script's own
                                 comment for the full story. Shipped as source (.te), not a
                                 precompiled .pp, so it's compiled against whatever policy version
                                 is actually running on the target host rather than baking in one
                                 specific version at build time. -->
                            <directory>/usr/share/nspawnmgr/selinux</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/selinux/nspawnmgr_machined_cgroup.te</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/nspawnmgr/guacamole-jdbc-drivers</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${guacamole.jdbc.drivers.dir}</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/lib/nspawnmgr/privileged</directory>
                            <filemode>750</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/privileged-scripts</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/lib/nspawnmgr</directory>
                            <filemode>750</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/setup-sudo-account.sh</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/install-guacamole-auth-jdbc.sh</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/install-guacamole-jdbc-drivers.sh</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/uninstall-nspawnmgr.sh</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/upgrade-nspawnmgr.sh</location></source>
                                <source><location>${debdir}/scripts/setup-ci-template-account.sh</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/etc/sudoers.d</directory>
                            <filemode>440</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <configuration>noreplace</configuration>
                            <directoryIncluded>false</directoryIncluded>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${staging.dir}/nspawnmgr_exec</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${staging.dir}/guacd-bundle-README.md</location></source>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <!-- Translated copies of the guide above - one subdirectory per language, English
                             stays canonical at the top level. -->
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/es</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/es/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/fr</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/fr/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/pt</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/pt/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/ja</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/ja/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/doc/nspawnmgr/zh</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../docs/zh/administrator-guide.md</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                        <!-- TranslationService's own source files - same "host-side fixed path, copied
                             into the machine's rootfs at bootstrap" pattern as everything else in
                             /usr/share/nspawnmgr above, see nspawnmgr-bootstrap-app-machine.sh's own
                             lang/ dir step. -->
                        <mapping>
                            <directory>/usr/share/nspawnmgr/lang</directory>
                            <filemode>644</filemode>
                            <username>root</username>
                            <groupname>root</groupname>
                            <sources>
                                <source><location>${project.basedir}/../../lang</location></source>
                            </sources>
                        </mapping>
                    </mappings>

                    <postinstallScriptlet>
                        <scriptFile>${project.basedir}/scripts/post-install.sh</scriptFile>
                        <fileEncoding>UTF-8</fileEncoding>
                    </postinstallScriptlet>
                    <!-- Deliberately no-op, matching ../nspawnmgr-arch/nspawnmgr.install's own
                         pre_remove()/post_remove() (see that file's own comment): never removes
                         the nspawnmgr_exec account, /etc/nspawnmgr, or the self-hosted machine -
                         those are the only credentials your containers stay reachable through.
                         Full cleanup is what the shared uninstall-nspawnmgr.sh script (staged
                         above at /usr/lib/nspawnmgr/uninstall-nspawnmgr.sh) is for, run by hand -
                         same posture as the .deb/Arch packages, not a gap specific to RPM. -->
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
